==Venue==
[[File:Puskás Aréna 01.jpg|thumb|275px|The [[Puskás Aréna]] in [[Budapest]] hosted the final.]]

The match was the first UEFA Cup/Europa League final to be held in [[Budapest]], and the second final in the competition's history to be held in Hungary after the [[1985 UEFA Cup final|1985 first leg]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0271-142efc51ba36-20a4481bdc54-1000--sevilla-vs-roma-in-the-2023-uefa-europa-league-final-all-y/ |title=Sevilla vs Roma in the 2023 UEFA Europa League final: All you need to know |date=31 May 2023 |work=[[UEFA]]|access-date=31 May 2023}}</ref>
The final was also the third UEFA club competition final to be held in the city after the [[2019 UEFA Women's Champions League final]] and the [[2020 UEFA Super Cup]],<ref name="ExCo 17 June"/> making it the fourth overall UEFA club final in Hungary. The stadium was also chosen as a venue for [[UEFA Euro 2020]], where it hosted three group stage matches and a round of 16 fixture.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/025b-0ed522c7a53a-5654a41fc442-1000/ |title=Budapest to stage 2022 UEFA Europa League final |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 March 2020 |access-date=2 March 2020}}</ref>

===Host selection===
The [[Puskás Aréna]] was selected as the final host by the UEFA Executive Committee during their meeting in [[Amsterdam]], the Netherlands, on 2 March 2020.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025a-0f8e7599aae5-eab7d48ee1f2-1000--uefa-executive-committee-agenda-for-amsterdam-meeting/ |title=UEFA Executive Committee agenda for Amsterdam meeting |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=27 February 2020 |access-date=27 February 2020}}</ref>

On 17 June 2020, the UEFA Executive Committee announced that due to the postponement and relocation of the [[2020 UEFA Europa League final|2020 final]], Budapest would instead host the 2023 final.<ref name="ExCo 17 June"/>

